county commissioners have adjusted how  they're spending coronavirus relief  funds  the board is now focusing some of the  federal dollars to increase kova testing  and provide  aid 8 news now reporter kristen drummond  attended that meeting  and filed this report clark county  commissioners voted to essentially  move around the cares act money they're  transferring funds from certain  categories  to address the biggest needs in our  community that motion passes  the board approving the reallocation in  a 6-0 vote  a presentation at the meeting shows a  breakdown of the 295 million dollars in  coronavirus relief funds  commissioners budgeted for certain items  months ago but  recent trends highlight some areas now  need more money than others  that includes financial assistance for  mortgage rent  and bills the county originally  designated 30 million dollars to that  line item  now there's nearly 59 million dollars  reallocated to that category from other  services  the county also reassigned two million  dollars towards cobit testing  when we started this in march we had no  idea what our expenses would be  so we put together a fluid plan that we  could  reorganize as as we got closer to see  what the real need was  county staff tell me 46 million dollars  has been spent so far  on rental assistance commissioners  encourage anyone needing help  to apply online for the clark county  cares housing assistance program  also known as chap before december 30th  that is the cut-off date to submit an  application for eligibility  we want to try and work to stabilize  folks we have a link to the housing  assistance page on our website  8newsnow.com  reporting by the clark county government  center kristen drummond 8 news now  and the county's cares act money must be  spent on expenses from the pandemic  accumulated  between march and the end of december
 

